# The adaptive conformance scene, depth half
# (docs/adaptive-layout-plan.md §2): the arrangement axis is a PROPERTY
# on one node with two constructor spellings. A HANDLER flips row@dash
# vertical and back — D2's user-driven toggle, the breakpoint
# mechanism's effect without its trigger (the breakpoint record is the
# milestone's next increment and drives this same prop).
#
# THE SUBJECT STAYS `row@dash` THROUGH BOTH STATES: identity is the
# creation kind, presentation is the prop — which is what keeps every
# other scene's addressing stable while an app adapts.
#
# expect_axis reads the axis the RENDER used (a geometry-adjacent
# record on the container's own box reader; GTK answers its orientable
# back), never the model's field — a backend that ignored the write
# must fail, the expect_aligned rule one prop over.
expect label#0 "alpha"
expect_axis row@dash "horizontal"
expect_axis column@steady "vertical"
click button#0
expect_axis row@dash "vertical"
# And BACK — the revert half a one-way flip would leave unproven.
click button#0
expect_axis row@dash "horizontal"
# THE BREAKPOINT HALF (docs/adaptive-layout-plan.md D3): row@narrow's axis
# is DECLARED data — below 520 points it is vertical — and THE CORE
# does the one comparison, so this is the same arithmetic on every
# platform. Every assert here sits AFTER a resize because the initial
# state is width-dependent (the panes-band rule): the desktop legs
# cross the threshold both ways, and the phone leg CUTS at
# resize_window and asserts its own always-narrow truth as a per-leg
# extra step (tools/ios/run-sim.sh's adaptive entry).
resize_window 480x400
expect_axis row@narrow "vertical"
# Crossing back AUTO-REVERTS — the diff-against-base rule, no second
# declaration anywhere.
resize_window 900x600
expect_axis row@narrow "horizontal"
